{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "Throwaway exploration optimizes for learning speed, so accepting AI-generated code judged only by whether it demonstrates the idea is appropriate. The cost of a wrong choice is near zero because the code is discarded anyway."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo to stakeholders or investors must reliably run in front of an audience, so behavior should be validated with tests even if line-by-line review is skipped. Selective review of the demo path catches the failures that would be most embarrassing."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Real early users make bugs visible and trust fragile, so every change should be read line by line to catch security, data, and correctness issues AI commonly introduces. The codebase will likely evolve into the production system, so early quality debt compounds."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "A maintained product with paying customers and a team needs code that humans fully understand and can safely modify, so AI should suggest while engineers author or rewrite every line. Line-by-line review alone is insufficient once multiple maintainers must own the design and intent."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 0,
      "rationale": "Regulated or safety-critical systems require auditable provenance, verifiable reasoning, and accountability that no AI-accepted code can currently satisfy. Human authorship every line keeps responsibility and compliance traceable to a person."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Allow AI autonomy to decrease monotonically as the cost of failure and the demand for maintainer understanding rise: full vibe coding (4) for throwaway prototypes, selective review plus tests (3) for demos, line-by-line review (2) for MVPs, human authorship guided by AI suggestions (1) for production, and no AI-written code (0) for critical systems. The driving principle is that the acceptable amount of unread AI code shrinks as the cost of a latent defect, the number of people who must understand the code, and the auditability requirements grow."
}
